PUBLIC RESPONSE: {A. A mutation in region 1 preventing its binding to region 2}

The trp leader transcript contains four segments capable of alternative base-pairing: 1-2 (pause), 2-3 (antiterminator), and 3-4 (intrinsic terminator). Under tryptophan-replete conditions the ribosome does not stall at the Trp codons inside segment 1, proceeds onto segment 2, and thereby blocks formation of the 2-3 antiterminator; segments 3 and 4 are therefore free to pair and terminate transcription.  

A mutation that abolishes 1-2 pairing leaves segment 2 unpaired even when the ribosome has cleared segment 1. Segment 2 is then available to pair with segment 3 as soon as segment 3 emerges, forming the antiterminator and thereby sequestering segment 3 so that the 3-4 terminator cannot form. Transcription therefore continues through the operon despite high tryptophan.  

Options B, C, D and E either favor terminator formation, alter termination efficiency without preventing hairpin formation, or reduce overall transcription; none of them block 3-4 pairing. Thus A is the only mutation that directly favors persistent 2-3 antiterminator formation under repressing conditions.}

